This guide covers everything you need to know about Git, from core architecture to day-to-day branching, merging, and remote collaboration workflows.

### 1. What is Git & Why Do We Use It?
Git is a distributed version control system (VCS) that tracks changes in your source code over time.

Version History: Allows you to revert to any previous state of your project if bugs occur.

Branching & Collaboration: Lets multiple developers work on separate features concurrently without overwriting each other's code.

Distributed Nature: Every developer has a full copy of the project repository, commit history, and branches locally on their machine.

### 2. Git Architecture: The Three Main Stages
Git tracks files through three distinct local stages before they reach a remote repository (like GitHub):

Plaintext
┌────────────────────────┐      git add       ┌──────────────────────┐     git commit     ┌────────────────────────┐
│    Working Directory   │ ─────────────────► │    Staging Area      │ ─────────────────► │    Local Repository    │
│  (Untracked/Modified)  │                    │       (Index)        │                    │      (Committed)       │
└────────────────────────┘                    └──────────────────────┘                    └────────────────────────┘
                                                                                                      │
                                                                                                      │ git push
                                                                                                      ▼
                                                                                          ┌────────────────────────┐
                                                                                          │   Remote Repository    │
                                                                                          │    (e.g., GitHub)      │
                                                                                          └────────────────────────┘
1. Working Directory: The actual files you see and edit in your file explorer or code editor. Files here are either untracked (new) or modified.

2. Staging Area (Index): The draft area. You selectively choose which changes should be included in the next snapshot.

3. Local Repository (.git folder): Stores the permanent snapshots (commits) locally on your machine.

4. Remote Repository: The hosted copy of your code on platforms like GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket.

### 3. Core Git Commands Cheat Sheet
Initializing & Configuring
Check current status:

Bash
git status
Shows untracked files, modified files, and staged changes.

Initialize a new Git repository:

Bash
git init
Creates a hidden .git folder in your project root.

Set your global user details:

Bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your.email@example.com"
Staging & Committing
Stage specific files:

Bash
git add app/main.py
Stage all files and directories in current folder:

Bash
git add .
Commit staged files with a descriptive message:

Bash
git commit -m "Add health check endpoint to Flask API"
View commit history:

Bash
git log --oneline
Branching & Switching
Branches allow you to develop features, fix bugs, or experiment in isolation without affecting the stable main branch.

Create and switch to a new branch:

Bash
git switch -c feature/login-page
# Or the legacy command:
git checkout -b feature/login-page
Switch between existing branches:

Bash
git switch main
# Or:
git checkout main
List all local branches:

Bash
git branch
Rename the current active branch (e.g., to main):

Bash
git branch -M main
Merging & Pull Requests
Merge a feature branch into the current active branch:

Bash
# Step 1: Switch to the destination branch
git switch main

# Step 2: Merge the feature branch
git merge feature/login-page
Delete a branch locally after merging:

Bash
git branch -d feature/login-page
Remote Repositories (Connecting to GitHub)
View currently linked remotes:

Bash
git remote -v
Add a new remote link (named origin):

Bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/<USERNAME>/<REPO-NAME>.git
Update an existing remote link:

Bash
git remote set-url origin https://github.com/<USERNAME>/<REPO-NAME>.git
Remove an existing remote:

Bash
git remote remove origin
Push code to the remote repository:

Bash
# First push (sets upstream tracking '-u'):
git push -u origin main

# Subsequent pushes:
git push
Pull latest changes from the remote repository:

Bash
git pull origin main
### 4. The .gitignore File
The .gitignore file tells Git which files, folders, and build artifacts it should completely ignore and never track.

Why is this essential?

Prevents sensitive secrets (e.g., .env files with API keys/passwords) from being committed.

Prevents heavy local dependencies and caches (e.g., venv/, node_modules/, __pycache__/) from cluttering your repository.

Example .gitignore:

Code snippet
# Python cache & virtual environments
__pycache__/
*.pyc
venv/
.pytest_cache/

# Environment files with secrets
.env

# OS-specific clutter
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
### 5. Typical Feature Branch Workflow (The Standard DevOps Pattern)
Plaintext
1. git switch -c feature/new-api-endpoint   # Create feature branch
2. # Write code and unit tests...
3. git add .                               # Stage changes
4. git commit -m "Implement endpoint"      # Create snapshot
5. git push -u origin feature/new-api-endpoint # Push branch to GitHub
6. Open a Pull Request (PR) on GitHub      # Automated CI tests run!
7. Code Review & Merge into 'main'         # Merged & automatically deployed!